WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.770
- [Instructor] Let's say
someone walks up to you

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.710
on the street, and they
give you this expression,

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:06.990
x squared plus seven x plus 10

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:10.300
divided by x plus two.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.550
And they say, "See if you
could simplify this thing."

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:16.610
And so pause this video,
and see if you can do that.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.972
And one way to think about it is what is

00:00:18.972 --> 00:00:22.450
x squared plus seven x plus
10 divided by x plus two?

00:00:22.450 --> 00:00:23.700
What is that going to be?

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.330
All right, now there's two ways
that you can approach this.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:29.800
One way is to try to factor the numerator

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.770
and see if it has a factor

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.390
that is common to the denominator.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.700
So let's try to do that.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.540
So we've done this many, many times.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:39.990
If this looks new to you,
I encourage you to review

00:00:39.990 --> 00:00:42.950
factoring polynomials other
places on Khan Academy.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.900
But what two numbers add up to seven

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.180
and when you multiply 'em you get 10?

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.790
Well, that would be two and five.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:52.240
So we could rewrite that
numerator as x plus two

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.600
times x plus five.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.070
And then, of course, the denominator,

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.090
you still have x plus two.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.800
And then we clearly see
we have a common factor.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.650
And so as long as x does
not equal negative two,

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:07.310
because if x equals negative two,

00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:09.170
this whole expression is undefined

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:11.140
because then you get a
zero in the denominator.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.430
So as long as x does
not equal negative two,

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.390
well, then we can divide the numerator

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.840
and the denominator by x plus two.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.470
Once again, the reason why
I put that constraint is

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.920
we can't divide the numerator
and the denominator by zero.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:25.920
So for any other values of x,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.950
this x plus two will be
nonzero, and we could divide

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.910
the numerator and the denominator by that.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:32.180
And they would cancel out,

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:36.540
and we would just be
left with x plus five.

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.000
So another way to think
about it is this expression,

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.230
our original expression,
could be viewed as x plus five

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:47.230
for any x that is not
equal to negative two.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:50.060
Now, the other way that
we could approach this

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.310
is through algebraic long division,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:55.640
which is very analogous to
the type of long division

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.060
that you might remember from,
I believe it was fourth grade.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:00.130
So what you do is you say,

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:03.500
all right, I'm going to
divide x plus two into

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.830
x squared plus seven x plus 10.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.740
And in this technique, you look
at the highest-degree term.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.620
So you have an x there
and an x squared there.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.310
You say, how many times
does x go into x squared?

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.120
Well, it goes x times.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.070
Now, you'd write that in this column

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:22.830
because x is just x to the first power.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:24.950
You could view this as
the first degree column.

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.890
It's analogous to the place values

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.570
that we talk about when
we first learn numbers

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:33.790
or how we regroup or about place value.

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.720
But here, you could
view it as degree places

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:36.740
or something like that.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:37.960
And then you take that x,

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:40.360
and you multiply it times
this entire expression.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.930
So x times two is two x.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.860
Put that in the first degree column.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.440
X times x is x squared.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.850
And then what we want to
do is we want to subtract

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.310
these things in yellow from
what we originally had in blue.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:55.960
So we could do it this way.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:02:57.910
And then we will be left with

00:02:57.910 --> 00:03:00.620
seven x minus two x is five x.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:03.930
And then x squared minus
x squared is just zero.

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.330
And then we can bring down this plus 10.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:11.080
And once again, we look at
the highest degree term.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.730
X goes into five x five times.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:14.660
That's a zero degree.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:17.750
It's a constant, so I'll write
it in the constant column.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.990
Five times two is 10.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.430
Five times x is five.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.520
And then I'll subtract these
from what we have up here.

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:27.540
And notice, we have no remainder.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.270
What's interesting about
algebraic long division,

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.180
we'll probably see it
in another video or two,

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:33.270
you can actually have a remainder.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.100
So those are going to
be situations where just

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:37.520
the factoring technique
alone would not have worked.

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.570
In this situation, this,

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.170
this model would have been easier,

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:43.500
but this is another way to think about it.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.860
You can say, hey, look, x
plus two times x plus five

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:47.860
is going to be equal to this.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.730
Now, if you wanted to
rewrite this expression

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.110
the way we did here and say,

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:55.000
hey, this expression is
equal to x plus five,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.230
we would have to constrain the domain.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.320
You'd say, hey, for all x's
not equaling negative two,

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.353
for these to be completely
identical expressions.

